Here is an implementation of a function that calculates the Damerau-Levenshtein Distance of two strings with a time complexity of O(m*n), where m is the length of str1 and n is the length of str2. The function handles cases where either str1 or str2 is an empty string, as well as cases where str1 and str2 contain special characters such as punctuation marks or whitespace:

```python
def damerau_levenshtein_distance(str1, str2):
    # Handle case where either str1 or str2 is empty
    if len(str1) == 0:
        return len(str2)
    if len(str2) == 0:
        return len(str1)

    # Create a matrix of size (len(str1)+1) x (len(str2)+1)
    matrix = [[0] * (len(str2) + 1) for _ in range(len(str1) + 1)]

    # Initialize the first row and first column of the matrix
    for i in range(len(str1) + 1):
        matrix[i][0] = i
    for j in range(len(str2) + 1):
        matrix[0][j] = j

    # Fill in the rest of the matrix
    for i in range(1, len(str1) + 1):
        for j in range(1, len(str2) + 1):
            if str1[i-1] == str2[j-1]:
                cost = 0
            else:
                cost = 1

            # Calculate the minimum cost from three adjacent cells
            deletion = matrix[i-1][j] + 1
            insertion = matrix[i][j-1] + 1
            substitution = matrix[i-1][j-1] + cost
            matrix[i][j] = min(deletion, insertion, substitution)

            # Check for transposition operation
            if i > 1 and j > 1 and str1[i-1] == str2[j-2] and str1[i-2] == str2[j-1]:
                transposition = matrix[i-2][j-2] + cost
                matrix[i][j] = min(matrix[i][j], transposition)

    # Return the final value in the bottom right cell of the matrix
    return matrix[len(str1)][len(str2)]

